---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import ContactSection from '../../components/ContactSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

// Wording is carried over from the current live page. Changes from the live page:
//  - "Who's liable" section rewritten to match the owner's reviewed fault wording: benefits do not
//    depend on fault, breaking safety rules or statutes can bar recovery, and a negligent third
//    party may be sued. The live page said the employer "is responsible for the actions of their
//    employees", which is not how the fault rule works. Needs Bryn's review.
//  - "injuries, pain, and wages" in the intro became "injuries and lost wages" (pain and suffering
//    is not payable under workers' comp, per the reviewed Workers' Comp page).
//  - Death benefits use the reviewed Workers' Comp page wording (no dollar figure, no remarriage rule).
//  - FAQ answers copied from the Factory page on the live site said "factory accident"; corrected to
//    "work accident". The "hearing within thirty days" promise is replaced with a plain statement
//    and the employer threshold now reads "three or more people". Needs Bryn's review.
//  - Photo from the old page left out (source unknown).
const title = 'Virginia Work-Related Car Accident Attorney | Pathfinder Injury Law';
const description =
  'Pathfinder Injury Law helps with Virginia work vehicle accidents. Get assistance now - call 804-505-0633 for your consultation.';
const h1 = 'Virginia Work-Related Car Accident Lawyer';
const path = '/car-accident-at-work-in-virginia/';

const toc = [
  { id: 'liable', label: 'Who’s Liable for a Car Accident at Work?' },
  { id: 'compensation', label: 'Compensation for a Virginia Car Accident at Work' },
  { id: 'denied', label: 'What If My Virginia Workers’ Comp Claim Is Denied?' },
  { id: 'help', label: 'Hurt in a Car Accident While Working?' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'Virginia Workers’ Comp Car Accident FAQ' },
];
---
<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Workers’ Compensation Lawyer Serving Virginia', path: '/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/' },
    { name: h1, path },
  ]}
  overlay
  author
>
  <PageHero
    title={h1}
    crumbs={[{ name: 'Workers’ Comp', href: '/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/' }, { name: 'Work Vehicle Accidents' }]}
  />

  <div class="wrap page-body">
    <article class="prose">
      <p class="lead">
        If you’re hurt in a car accident at work, you need Workers’ Comp benefits from your employer.
        Workers’ comp can pay for your medical treatment and part of your lost wages.
      </p>
      <p>
        For many of us, driving is part of the job. Whether you’re a delivery driver, a truck driver, or
        driving is only a small part of your job, you may spend hours behind the wheel every week.
      </p>
      <p>Sadly, that means you’re at risk for a serious car accident at work in Virginia.</p>
      <p>
        Here’s how <a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/">workers’ comp benefits</a> work after a crash on the job.
      </p>

      <h2 id="liable">Who’s Liable for a Car Accident at Work?</h2>
      <p>
        When you’re hurt at work in Virginia, one of your first concerns may be about who caused your
        injuries and who should pay for them. Someone should be held responsible for their actions,
        right?
      </p>
      <p>
        Because you’re facing a <a href="/how-to-file-a-workers-comp-claim/">Workers’ Comp claim</a>,
        you generally don’t have to prove who caused the accident. Even if your own mistake caused it,
        you can usually still recover benefits. However, a willful failure to use a safety appliance or perform a statutory duty, or a willful breach of a known, reasonable rule of the employer, can bar recovery. The employer has the burden of proof.
      </p>
      <p>
        But liability can impact your
        <a href="/what-happens-when-you-file-a-workers-comp-claim/">Workers’ Comp claim</a>. For
        example, if another driver caused the crash, you may also be able to bring a personal injury
        claim against that driver, so long as they are not a coworker or in the same trade, business,
        or occupation as your employer. Workers’ compensation is generally the exclusive remedy against your employer, so a lawsuit against your employer is generally barred except in extremely rare situations. Claims against third parties may exist, such as suing the driver of another vehicle.
      </p>

      <div class="pull">
        <p><strong>Learn how we can help you with your vehicle accident Workers’ Comp case.</strong></p>
        <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#contact">Get Help Now</a></p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="compensation">Compensation for a Virginia Car Accident at Work</h2>
      <p>
        Hurt in a serious car accident while working in Virginia? You’ve got a shot at getting
        compensated for your injuries through your Workers’ Comp benefits. The problem is, you may have
        some questions about what these benefits really cover and what you can receive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Below is just a sample of what you can expect from your Workers’ Comp benefits.
      </p>

      <h3>Medical Costs</h3>
      <p>
        A Virginia car accident can cause some severe injuries, from back injuries to
        <a href="/concussion-brain-injury-workers-comp/">concussions</a> and
        more. Because of this, you may already have medical bills piling up. Worse, you don’t have the
        funds to pay for them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The insurance company is required to pay for treatment that is
        reasonable, necessary, and causally related to your work accident, including travel,
        rehabilitation, and specialist care. That doesn’t mean it will.
      </p>

      <h3>Disability Benefits</h3>
      <p>
        It’s not easy to recover if you don’t have the money to pay for your recovery. Your medical
        bills may be covered, but what about the costs to keep the lights on and food on the table for
        your family? Luckily, that’s where your disability benefits kick in.
      </p>
      <p>
        Disability benefits replace a portion of the income you lost. What percentage of your original
        wages you’ll get depends on the severity of your injuries and whether you
        can return to work.
        For example, if you are able to work in some way, the weekly amount of your benefits may change, or other legal requirements may arise, such as searching for work.
      </p>

      <h3>Benefits for Fatal Accidents</h3>
      <p>
        If you’ve lost a loved one in a fatal car accident at their Virginia work site, you may still
        receive Workers’ Comp benefits for your loss. Losing a loved one is always difficult, but when
        you rely on them for financial support, you’re facing more than grief: you’re facing serious
        financial hardship tied to your loss.
      </p>
      <p>
        Family members may receive death benefits for their losses. These benefits can pay for a
        limited amount of funeral expenses, medical bills incurred before the death, and lost wages for
        certain periods of time for certain survivors or dependents who meet statutory criteria.
      </p>

      <h2 id="denied">What If My Virginia Workers’ Comp Claim Is Denied?</h2>
      <p>
        You may have been waiting by the mailbox for the letter offering you Workers’ Comp benefits. But
        when you opened it, you had only a denial letter in your hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, don’t panic. A denial is not the end of the claim. Start with the reason the insurance
        company gives for denying it. That reason is a good first look at what your evidence will
        have to answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can ask for a hearing before the Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission. At the hearing,
        a deputy commissioner hears the evidence and decides whether benefits are due.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/how-to-appeal-a-workers-comp-denial-in-virginia/">Workers’ Comp Denials</a></p>

      <div class="pull" id="help">
        <h2>Hurt in a Car Accident While Working?</h2>
        <p>
          A crash on the job can mean a workers’ comp claim and, sometimes, a claim against the other
          driver. <a href="/">Pathfinder Injury Law</a> handles only Virginia workers’ compensation claims.
          Call <a href={site.phoneHref}>804-505-0633</a> or fill out our
          <a href="#contact">online form</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="faq">Virginia Workers’ Comp Car Accident FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>When can I expect my benefits from my accident?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              When you’re hurt in a Virginia work accident, you need Workers’ Comp benefits now, not
              later. You should receive confirmation that your claim has been accepted or denied as soon
              as possible. If your claim is denied, you have the right to request a hearing before the
              Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission so you don’t have to keep waiting for your
              Workers’ Comp coverage.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/how-long-do-virginia-workers-comp-settlements-take/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does Virginia Workers’ Comp pay for lost wages?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              After a serious work injury in Virginia, you may be directed to your Workers’ Comp
              benefits to recover from your injuries. You can’t cover your bills and needs without your
              wages, so it’s vital that you get coverage.
            </p>
            <p>
              Yes, in part. If the insurance company accepts your claim for lost wages, it sends you an
              Award Agreement. Once that is signed and the Commission enters an award, you are paid for
              the time you are unable to work because of the injury, starting on day 8.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/virginia-workers-comp-lawyer/does-virginia-workers-comp-pay-for-lost-wages/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>In Virginia, can I get all my Workers’ Comp benefits right now?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              In some cases, you may want a
              <a href="/should-i-accept-a-lump-sum-settlement-for-my-workers-comp-claim/">lump-sum settlement</a>
              for your Virginia work accident, rather than weekly payments. A lump sum means you’ll
              receive a compromised payment of your benefits all at once. Settling your case closes
              everything and ends benefits, so you may be unable to get further benefits for your injury if you’re not healing as fast as your doctors thought.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What happens when you file a Workers’ Comp claim?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              After a serious work accident, you may have immediately reported your accident to your
              employer. They need to know about injuries that happen at work, and they’re responsible
              for your Workers’ Comp benefits.
            </p>
            <p>
              After you file, the Commission will order your employer’s insurance company to respond to
              your claim within 30 days. It may accept the claim or deny it.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/what-happens-when-you-file-a-workers-comp-claim/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What’s the Workers’ Comp Process in Virginia?</summary>
          <div class="answer">
            <p>
              Virtually all employers with three or more employees regularly in service in Virginia are required to carry workers’ compensation insurance. If an employee receives an injury while completing their job-related duties, they can file a claim with the Virginia Workers’ Compensation Commission to seek benefits including reimbursement of costs. Employees can use the Workers’ Comp process to apply for several
              financial benefits.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/workers-comp-process-in-virginia/">Learn More</a></p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </article>

    <aside class="side" aria-label="Page navigation and contact">
      <div class="side-card side-call">
        <h2>Free Consultation</h2>
        <a class="cta-phone" href={site.phoneHref}>{site.phoneDisplay}</a>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#contact">Get Help Now</a>
      </div>
      <nav class="side-card" aria-label="On this page">
        <h2>On This Page</h2>
        <ul class="toc">
          {toc.map((t) => <li><a href={`#${t.id}`}>{t.label}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <ContactSection />
</Base>
